# Line endings. WHY THIS EXISTS:
#
# This repo had no .gitattributes at all, so 116 config blobs were committed
# with CRLF - whatever the machine that last touched them happened to use. The
# server repo DOES normalise (`* text=auto`), so every file synced between the
# two flipped its line endings and showed up as fully rewritten, over and over.
#
# `text=auto` makes the repo canonical: LF inside git, and on checkout the
# platform's native ending - CRLF on Windows, LF on Linux. Forge on Windows then
# rewrites configs with the CRLF that is already there, so the bytes match and
# `git status` stays clean.
#
# If a Windows clone ever shows hundreds of "modified" files again with an empty
# `git diff`, the fix is `git add --renormalize .` - it stages nothing, edits no
# file, and clears the stale stat cache. Never `git reset --hard` for this.
* text=auto

# Never touch these - converting a binary corrupts it. `text=auto` already
# detects most binaries by content, but a file that happens to contain no NUL
# bytes would slip through, so the known types are pinned explicitly.
*.png    binary
*.so     binary
*.pyc    binary
*.jar    binary
*.zip    binary
*.rar    binary
*.ogg    binary
*.nbt    binary
*.dat    binary
*.schem  binary
*.mca    binary
*.mcpack binary
